}
</style>
<small>shape: (136_255, 3)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>City</th><th>Volume_Gal</th><th>Buckets</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>f64</td><td>i64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;DECORAH&quot;</td><td>271.83</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;DECORAH&quot;</td><td>269.06</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;DECORAH&quot;</td><td>269.06</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;DECORAH&quot;</td><td>269.06</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;DECORAH&quot;</td><td>269.05</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>&hellip;</td><td>&hellip;</td><td>&hellip;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;DECORAH&quot;</td><td>0.01</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;DECORAH&quot;</td><td>0.01</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;DECORAH&quot;</td><td>0.01</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;DECORAH&quot;</td><td>0.0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;DECORAH&quot;</td><td>0.0</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


1. Determines the number of buckets to be used, in this case 100.

Reviewing the output for Decorah, the five largest transactions, all above 250 gallons, fall into the 100th percentile. A small value such as 0.01 gallons appears in the first percentile, and the minimum values of 0.0 also occupy that lowest bucket. Once these bucket assignments are in place, we can compute the upper and lower bounds of each *Bucket* using `max` and `min`. To keep the example concise, we will use four buckets, although any positive integer can be used in the `mul` function.

::: {#1dc11fe6 .cell execution_count=9}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(liquor_sales
 .filter(pl.col('Volume_Gal').ge(0),
         pl.col('City') == 'DECORAH')
 .with_columns(pl.col('Volume_Gal')
      .rank('ordinal')
      .over('City')
      .mul(4)
      .truediv(pl.len().over('City'))
      .ceil()
      .cast(pl.Int64)
      .alias('Buckets'))
 .group_by('City','Buckets')
 .agg(Minimum=pl.min('Volume_Gal'),
      Maximum=pl.max('Volume_Gal'),)
 .sort('City','Buckets', descending=[False, True])
)
