We observe that 2012 and 2013 had relatively few liquor transactions compared to later years. From 2014 onward, transaction counts increased steadily through 2019. In 2020, the number of transactions rose sharply. One possible explanation is that during the pandemic, people spent more time at home and may have consumed more alcohol. Another possibility is a data issue, such as duplicated records. To investigate further, we can examine the data at a monthly level:

::: {#61a8fc67 .cell execution_count=29}
``` {.python .cell-code}
to_plot = (liquor_sales
 .filter(pl.col('Volume_Gal').ge(0))
 .with_columns(Transaction_Month=pl.col('Date').dt.truncate('1mo').cast(pl.Date))
 .group_by('Transaction_Month')
 .agg(Transactions=pl.len())
 .sort(pl.nth(0))
)
to_plot
```

